I'm Evan G borin I am the faculty  director of the center prior art science  and technology it's great to see you all  here and I mean that both in the general  sense and in the specific sense of those  of you who came back to to hear the  following performance this is actually  the first half of the quote-unquote  audible panel that joins the other more  traditional panels but since it involves  music we just thought it would be really  nice to just have the audible panel be  music itself so we have two really  incredible creative musicians who are  going to be performing their work for  you later on at 6:15 my advisor will be  performing her collaboration with  Michael Harrison and bill Morrison  called just aims and loops but I want to  introduce grace Leslie to you now in  terms of the theme of this conference  being material we tried to think about  ways in which material in the musical  sense could be rethought of other than  in the oh let's let's bring out new  innovations in stuff being used to  generate music and really get back to  the fundamentals of the materials of  music oddly enough my introduction to  experimental music came when I was 13  years old and I had this incredible  music teacher in high school who taught  a class called materials of music and in  this class materials of music we learned  about everything other than material we  learned about these malleable objects  and shapes and formal designs that were  all completely abstract they all had to  do with things like melodies and the  form of music etc etc and one of the  things that she Betty Jacobson first  introduced me to when I had barely hit  adolescence was the work of Alvin Lucier  and so she kind of blew our minds by  playing I'm sitting in a room to a to a  bunch of 13-year old sitting in a room  and of course Alvin was our guest  musician two years ago at our first  symposium and he performed I am sitting  in a room and he also talked about his  piece music for solo performer which was  one of the earliest and most successful  and certainly groundbreaking pieces that  involved using brain waves to generate  music and so it seemed really natural  that for this brain waves being the most  latent material that I think could  possibly generate music to bring that up  to date 2017 by bringing somebody else  who's making music with brain waves and  that is Grace Leslie she's currently a  visiting scientist at the Media Lab  she's a composer and a researcher she  will be a Newcombe fellow at Dartmouth  next year and then she will take a  faculty position at Georgia Tech those  are her credentials for her daily  affirmation but I want to just say a  little bit more about what she's going  to be doing so this also relates to  Nicholas Negroponte whose comments both  his final comments first about the  natural and the artificial becoming one  because I'll get back to that but also  his brief comment about biotech as the  digital of the future because in  addition to Grace's research work and  her musical work she is very involved in  using music both in traditional forms  and in terms of music generated by brain  waves in therapeutic situations and  working toward wellness so that directly  relates to what Nicholas was telling us  but in terms of natural and artificial  what grace does is that she's going to  be generating the music that you hear  basically by three means one of them the  most traditional means of making music  which is taking a physical object and  causing it to vibrate the air around it  by blowing through it and that would be  her on her flute simultaneously she's  measuring her own brain activity and and  her brainwaves are in turn triggering  synthesized and sampled sounds that  she's also created so she's taking the  material of music being pushing air  pressure around the room in various ways  at its absolutely most molecular and  wave-like way and this is her piece  vessels  [Applause]
 

